// ora-mod-fa — generated by tools/merge.js: src/ora-mod-fa-*.js plus matching questions from the Oracle role banks (tools/modules.js).
QB['ora-mod-fa'] = (QB['ora-mod-fa'] || []).concat([["Fixed Assets","How would you architect asset accounting for a capital-intensive company with projects?","- **Capital projects** in Fusion Projects with capitalization rules (asset grouping, CIP).\n- **Assets** module with **corporate and tax books**; IFRS book if needed.\n- **Asset tracking** (physical) via Maintenance or third-party (tags, locations).\n- **Lease accounting** for leased assets (ROU).\n- **Impairment** and revaluation processes.\n- **Integration** with maintenance (work orders) for component-level tracking if required.\n- Close: depreciation runs, CIP aging review, reconciliation to GL.","D"],["Fixed Assets","How does an asset get created from an AP invoice?","- An AP invoice line is charged to an **asset clearing account** (or flagged as track as asset).\n- After accounting, run **Create Mass Additions** — this sends eligible lines to the **Mass Additions** queue in Assets.\n- In Assets, **review** the mass additions: assign category, location, cost center, date in service, merge or split lines.\n- **Post Mass Additions** creates the assets.\n- Depreciation starts based on the date placed in service and the prorate convention.","B"],["Fixed Assets","What are the main asset transactions in Oracle Assets?","- **Addition** — new asset.\n- **Adjustment** — change cost, life, method.\n- **Transfer** — move to another location, cost center or employee.\n- **Reclassification** — change asset category.\n- **Retirement** — full or partial, with proceeds of sale and cost of removal; gain/loss calculated.\n- **Reinstatement** — undo a retirement in the same period.\n- **Revaluation** and **impairment** where required.\n\nEach creates accounting entries through SLA after Create Accounting.","B"],["Fixed Assets","What is depreciation prorate convention?","The prorate convention decides **when depreciation starts** for an asset based on its date placed in service — e.g.:\n- **Following month** — starts next month.\n- **Mid-month** — half a month in the month added.\n- **Actual month** — full month in the month added.\n- **Half-year** (common in US tax books) — half a year of depreciation in the first year regardless of month.\n\nIt's set on the asset category per book and copied to the asset.","D"],["Fixed Assets","What happens when you retire an asset?","- Choose full or partial retirement, date, proceeds of sale and cost of removal.\n- Oracle calculates **gain or loss** = proceeds − (cost − accumulated depreciation) − removal cost.\n- Run **Calculate Gains and Losses**, then create accounting: removes cost and accumulated depreciation, books proceeds and gain/loss.\n- A retirement can be reinstated in the same period if done by mistake.","D"],["Fixed Assets","Explain how asset books, categories and depreciation work in Oracle Assets, including tax books.","- **Corporate book** — the primary book linked to a ledger, holding the main depreciation for financial reporting.\n- **Tax books** — copies of corporate assets with different depreciation methods/lives (e.g. MACRS in the US); kept in step using **Mass Copy** each period.\n- **Asset categories** — default the accounts, depreciation method, life and prorate convention per book.\n- **Depreciation** runs per book per period; once run and the period closed it creates accounting through SLA.\n- **Additions** come from manual entry, Mass Additions from AP invoices, or projects (CIP to capitalized assets).\n\n## Things I watch\n- Category design matches both financial and tax needs.\n- Mass Additions from AP are reviewed weekly, not at month-end.\n- Retirements, transfers and impairments flow correctly to GL.\n\nTip: If you've done IFRS vs local GAAP, mention multiple corporate books or secondary ledgers for different depreciation.","D"],["Fixed Assets","How would you design Oracle Assets for a company with IFRS and US GAAP plus tax books?","- **Corporate book** linked to the primary ledger (e.g. US GAAP).\n- **Tax books** (federal, state) with MACRS methods via mass copy.\n- For IFRS differences (component depreciation, revaluation, different lives): a second **corporate book** linked to a **secondary ledger**, or **adjustment-only** approach for few differences.\n- **Category** design that supports both bases.\n- **Impairment** and revaluation processes for IFRS.\n- Reconcile each book to its ledger every period.","D"],["Fixed Assets","How do you handle construction-in-progress (CIP) assets?","- Costs are collected as **CIP assets** (from AP mass additions or Projects).\n- CIP doesn't depreciate.\n- When construction is complete, **capitalize** the CIP asset — it becomes a capitalized asset with a date placed in service and starts depreciating.\n- With Fusion Projects, use **capital projects** to build assets, generate asset lines and interface them to Assets.\n\nWatch: CIP aging review so completed projects get capitalized on time.","D"],["Fixed Assets","How do you merge or split mass additions?","- **Merge** combines several invoice lines into one asset (e.g. freight and installation added to the main equipment).\n- **Split** turns one line with quantity 10 into 10 assets (e.g. 10 laptops).\n- **Add to existing asset** — cost adjustments to an asset already in service.\n- Done in the **Mass Additions** work area before posting.\n- Merged lines inherit the parent's category and details.","B"],["Lease Accounting","What do you know about Oracle Fusion Lease Accounting?","Supports **ASC 842 / IFRS 16** for lessees:\n- Lease contracts with payments, terms and options.\n- Calculates the **right-of-use asset** and **lease liability** (present value of payments at the discount rate).\n- Classification (finance vs operating under ASC 842).\n- Generates amortization schedules, accounting journals, and **AP invoices** for payments.\n- Integrates with Assets (ROU assets) and AP.\n- Handles modifications, impairments and terminations.","D"]]);
